WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.568
NICOLE STEELE:
<i> So these cops,</i>

00:00:02.601 --> 00:00:05.671
<i> caught lying and with
 their abusive behavior...</i>

00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:07.607
remained on the street?

00:00:09.909 --> 00:00:11.077
We own this city.

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:14.114
♪ (DRAMATIC MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:16.683 line:0%
The story of<i> We Own This City</i>

00:00:16.716 --> 00:00:18.617 line:0%
is about
the Gun Trace Task Force,

00:00:18.684 --> 00:00:20.853
<i> which was probably
 one of the most corrupt squads</i>

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:22.555
<i>in policing history in America.</i>

00:00:23.722 --> 00:00:25.925
SPEAKER:<i> The actual police work
 of protecting a community,</i>

00:00:25.959 --> 00:00:28.560
<i> of solving crimes,
 had collapsed.</i>

00:00:29.061 --> 00:00:30.463 line:0%
That was already critiqued
in<i> The Wire.</i>

00:00:30.964 --> 00:00:33.399 line:0%
The next moment
in the history of Baltimore

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:35.601
<i> is that officers were actually
 just robbing people.</i>

00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:37.203
-OFFICER: How much you got here?
-Eleven thousand.

00:00:37.236 --> 00:00:38.138
OFFICER 2: (WHISTLES) Wow.

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:39.972
That's the equity from
when I refinanced my house.

00:00:40.039 --> 00:00:41.473
"The equity" my ass.

00:00:41.774 --> 00:00:43.476
A lot of thought went into,
how do we do this

00:00:43.542 --> 00:00:44.877
in the most careful way?

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:46.546
When did you first start
doing robberies?

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:48.314
It wasn't like armed robbery.

00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:53.386 line:0%
Every story in this series
was based on

00:00:53.419 --> 00:00:54.620 line:0%
some real-life, true event.

00:00:54.654 --> 00:00:55.889
<i> You can feel that on set.</i>

00:00:56.322 --> 00:00:58.091
<i> It gives it a different level
 of attention to detail,</i>

00:00:58.124 --> 00:01:00.393
<i> a different level
 of authenticity.</i>

00:01:00.426 --> 00:01:03.128 line:0%
To see it on screen
is gonna be triggering

00:01:03.162 --> 00:01:04.663 line:0%
for a lot of people.

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:06.966
Get on the wall.
There's always one watching.

00:01:06.999 --> 00:01:09.502
D WATKINS:<i> The tradeoff is
 it has to be shown</i>

00:01:09.568 --> 00:01:13.072
<i> because it's the only way
 we'll ever get this reform</i>

00:01:13.106 --> 00:01:15.074
<i> that everybody
 dreams about.</i>

00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:17.409
JON BERNTHAL:
<i> Shooting this in Baltimore,</i>

00:01:17.443 --> 00:01:18.844
<i>you can't ask for anything more.</i>

00:01:18.911 --> 00:01:21.013 line:0%
We have to tell this story here,
with these folks,

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:22.648 line:0%
for these folks,
and by these folks.

00:01:22.681 --> 00:01:25.084
(TIRES SCREECHING)

00:01:25.651 --> 00:01:27.519
-OFFICER 3: Show me your hands!
-OFFICER 4: Get out of the car!

00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:29.989 line:0%
It happened in Baltimore,
and we're here

00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:31.357 line:0%
walking in their shoes now.

00:01:31.757 --> 00:01:34.127
<i> Meeting people
 that they affected.</i>

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:35.361
CIVILIAN: Why you pull me over
anyway?

00:01:35.828 --> 00:01:37.496
Well, why did we pull him over?

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:39.365
Rear license obscured.

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:41.868
It don't look obscured to me.

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:47.173 line:0%
But it was quite emotional
to be hearing the stories

00:01:47.206 --> 00:01:50.510
<i> from passersby telling us
 what it felt like</i>

00:01:50.543 --> 00:01:53.346
<i> and what hasn't changed
 and what needs to change.</i>

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:54.647
Police yourselves!

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:55.881
REINALDO GREEN:
<i> My father worked for</i>

00:01:55.948 --> 00:01:57.717
<i>the Department of Investigation
 in New York,</i>

00:01:57.784 --> 00:02:00.353
<i> so I have a lot of respect
 for a lot of the practices,</i>

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:02.521
but disdain for some
of the institutional things.

00:02:02.554 --> 00:02:04.457
And so, I was sort of
always in the middle.

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:06.525
<i> Those nuances, hopefully,</i>

00:02:06.559 --> 00:02:08.561
<i> make their way into
 the storytelling process</i>

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:11.030
because these are human beings
put in real-life situations.

00:02:11.063 --> 00:02:12.564
<i> And what would you do
 if you were put</i>

00:02:12.631 --> 00:02:13.566
<i> in that situation?</i>

00:02:13.632 --> 00:02:14.734
OFFICER 5: I don't know, man.

00:02:15.534 --> 00:02:17.036
OFFICER 6:
What you don't know, man?
It's yours, you earned it.

00:02:17.069 --> 00:02:19.005
JOSH CHARLES:<i> You have all these
 different points of view.</i>

00:02:19.038 --> 00:02:20.973 line:0%
And every point of view
is given some validity.

00:02:21.006 --> 00:02:23.375
<i> It's really easy to just
 make this guy the bad guy</i>

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:24.877
<i> and these guys
 are the good guys.</i>

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:26.512
<i> And on some level,
 that is what's happening.</i>

00:02:26.546 --> 00:02:28.247
<i> But there's just
 more nuance to it.</i>

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.749
If you want to do this job,
then you're gonna get complaints

00:02:30.816 --> 00:02:31.884
for doing this job.

00:02:32.351 --> 00:02:35.387 line:0%
This story is so relevant
and will remain relevant

00:02:35.421 --> 00:02:38.223 line:0%
until something actually
happens to change that.

00:02:38.991 --> 00:02:40.526
JUSTIN FENTON:<i> We're trying
 to explore the tension</i>

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.528 line:0%
between trying
to keep the city safe

00:02:42.561 --> 00:02:44.063 line:0%
and doing it
in a constitutional way.

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:45.564 line:0%
I think we're still trying
to figure that out.

00:02:45.831 --> 00:02:47.232
It's not a partisan issue.

00:02:47.266 --> 00:02:50.770
<i> If policing's fair,
 then it's good for all of us.</i>

00:02:51.104 --> 00:02:53.439
♪ (DRAMATIC OUTRO
MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

